  <title>6.7.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01695</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3390eceb-d907-4855-bac1-1496803a72e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To summarize the key concepts from the lesson, pose these sample questions again to see if students have learned all of these situations:</para>
<list>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to factoring binomials? (difference of squares; factoring the GCF) </item>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx +c\)? (undoing FOIL) </item>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx +c\)? (trial and error, the “\(ac\)” method) </item>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to factoring trinomials of the form \(ax^2 \pm 2ab + b^2\)? (perfect square trinomials) </item>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to factoring polynomials with 4 expressions? (grouping) </item>
  <item> Which strategy is applicable to polynomials that don’t seem to factor easily? (substitution, grouping, factoring the GCF) </item>
</list></section>
</content>
